1
00:00:07,658 --> 00:00:08,218
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the question?

2
00:00:08,378 --> 00:00:09,439
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at the point of view...

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:23,264
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anicca applies to the point of view, which you said, rather than to things like your book, or your lamp, or your... Yeah, yeah.

4
00:00:23,945 --> 00:00:24,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a leaf.

5
00:00:24,545 --> 00:00:24,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

6
00:00:25,105 --> 00:00:26,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to apply that...

7
00:00:26,606 --> 00:00:32,028
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to apply, or rather you can say anicca can apply to both, through the point of view.

8
00:00:33,419 --> 00:00:53,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to apply anicca to the things, as it sometimes might have been done, you need to... you have to apply it only to those things upon which your view depends, upon which your sense of self depends, upon the which of your center of... upon the which the center of your experience depends.

9
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:57,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, upon things that you took as mine.

10
00:00:58,273 --> 00:01:14,526
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not... Anicca will never be understood in its... in its... in its... in its nature, if you try to approach it, think about it, as a... as a some sort of law of physics or whatever, whereby everything is impermanent.

11
00:01:14,646 --> 00:01:17,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, everything is constantly changing.

12
00:01:17,208 --> 00:01:18,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, everything will come to an end.

13
00:01:19,369 --> 00:01:26,415
[SPEAKER_01]: That might be true in theoretical sense, but in terms of freeing you from suffering, that understanding simply won't do.

14
00:01:30,923 --> 00:01:40,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Because anicca results in freedom from suffering when you see your own sense of self, your own center of experience undermined by it.

15
00:01:40,851 --> 00:01:50,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at the random things that you don't care, you have absolutely no emotional connection to whatsoever, like, you simply... they're simply irrelevant to you.

16
00:01:50,638 --> 00:01:53,781
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... you can... like, you can see them as impermanent as much as you like.

17
00:01:54,021 --> 00:01:55,162
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't make a big difference.

18
00:01:56,757 --> 00:01:57,817
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

19
00:01:57,897 --> 00:02:04,280
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said before, people associate impermanence with witnessing of the destruction of a thing.

20
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:06,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Witnessing its ending means, ah, it's impermanent.

21
00:02:07,141 --> 00:02:09,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can never be applied to your own point of view.

22
00:02:09,922 --> 00:02:14,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would require you to see your own seeing as impermanent.

23
00:02:14,904 --> 00:02:18,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, in order to see that, you have to see your own seeing ending.

24
00:02:18,565 --> 00:02:20,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see the end of your own seeing.

25
00:02:21,126 --> 00:02:23,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't make... well, it's impossible.

26
00:02:24,392 --> 00:02:32,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that would require you to step outside of the experience as a whole, the five aggregates, and then see them disappear, and then say, okay, they are impermanent.

27
00:02:32,938 --> 00:02:36,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's that's simply inconceivable, because you can't step outside of them.

28
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:37,961
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there is no outside of them.

29
00:02:38,161 --> 00:02:42,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you think about outside of the five aggregates, that experience as a whole is within the five aggregates.

30
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:59,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then it becomes clear that the impermanence that the Buddha was teaching about, that does pertain to the five aggregates and the freedom from the whole thing, cannot possibly be the impermanence of, of, oh, I just looked, I just seen a thing being destroyed, thus it's impermanent.

31
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:01,048
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a worldly impermanence.

32
00:03:02,369 --> 00:03:07,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, as I think we discussed before, anicca,

33
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:14,813
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more than just what's thought as impermanence, which means you should probably find a better translation for it.

34
00:03:15,553 --> 00:03:24,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, whatever translation is found, they probably need to be explained heavily, because people already have deeply rooted notions of impermanence, permanence, or whatever else.

35
00:03:25,058 --> 00:03:26,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything's impermanent.

36
00:03:26,759 --> 00:03:27,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, everything is impermanent.

37
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:28,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is.

38
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,602
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't really free you from suffering, that kind of understanding.

39
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:41,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, if it's not free from suffering, it means it's not the impermanence that the Buddha was teaching about, that directly results in the recognition of not-self.

40
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:57,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anicca is closer related to the lack of control over things, lack of mastery, than it's related to the common notion of impermanence, of witnessing a thing's ending.

41
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:24,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, by saying, by experiencing that point of view, by seeing it phenomenologically, you can't say that it is permanent or impermanent, in the sense of, no, because

42
00:04:25,335 --> 00:04:29,838
[SPEAKER_00]: There would just be an assumption, because you would have to step out of that point of view to be able to see the flow.

43
00:04:29,858 --> 00:04:36,382
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have to fall away from the phenomenology, from the mindfulness of that experience.

44
00:04:36,563 --> 00:04:52,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you call it permanent, even if you call it eternal, you will have to fall away from that experience, because exactly, yeah, permanent or impermanent, it's applied to it secondarily, as an assumption on top of it, while in reality, what your experience shows you, it's basically

45
00:04:56,123 --> 00:04:58,524
[SPEAKER_01]: There is that center of that experience, and that's it.

46
00:04:58,724 --> 00:04:58,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

47
00:04:59,104 --> 00:05:02,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a sense of infinite, always being there.

48
00:05:02,485 --> 00:05:03,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Always being there.

49
00:05:03,666 --> 00:05:12,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Infinite, kind of, infinite, sort of, appearance, in the terms of the infinite possibilities, which means infinite possibilities of being there, which means being there infinitely.

50
00:05:13,309 --> 00:05:16,310
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is then confused as lasting forever.

51
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:22,272
[SPEAKER_01]: But no matter how far the thing reaches, like, there is no end in sight.

52
00:05:22,372 --> 00:05:24,833
[SPEAKER_01]: If that whole structure is swept away,

53
00:05:25,775 --> 00:05:27,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Means none of it can remain standing.

54
00:05:28,718 --> 00:05:34,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you don't apply the idea of anicca to things individually, or things that you can gather through your senses.

55
00:05:34,663 --> 00:05:37,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You apply it to the structure of your senses.

56
00:05:37,366 --> 00:05:37,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

57
00:05:37,906 --> 00:05:39,007
[SPEAKER_01]: The point of view and...

58
00:05:40,749 --> 00:05:42,070
[SPEAKER_01]: The very root of it, yeah.

59
00:05:42,531 --> 00:05:51,259
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's abandoned on that level, through seeing that you have no control, no mastery over it, you have certainly nothing to do with its arising, you realize, for that reason,

60
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:56,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't even say, for that reason, it will come to an end.

61
00:05:56,452 --> 00:06:00,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, it will induce people to think, oh, so I will witness the end.

62
00:06:00,634 --> 00:06:01,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive that.

63
00:06:02,315 --> 00:06:05,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, better to say, for that reason, it will never be mine.

64
00:06:07,617 --> 00:06:17,101
[SPEAKER_01]: When you see it as truly impermanent, as truly arisen on its own, independent of you, you're absolutely... it's inconceivable for you to have any say in it.

65
00:06:17,782 --> 00:06:18,442
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there.

66
00:06:19,353 --> 00:06:25,137
[SPEAKER_01]: For that reason, it will never ever be yours, even in a remote, the wireless or the fantasy.

67
00:06:25,157 --> 00:06:31,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even access it, even in that theoretical, like, idea of ownership.

68
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:32,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

69
00:06:33,183 --> 00:06:39,868
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that realization pervades your mind, through and through and through and through and through, you will simply stop appropriating anything.

70
00:06:40,168 --> 00:06:47,293
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what arises, on what level, in what sense, how, what experience, it's just... the idea of taking it up is gone.

71
00:06:48,172 --> 00:06:49,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all it takes.

72
00:06:49,533 --> 00:06:51,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the point of view is basically...

73
00:06:51,373 --> 00:06:59,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when nothing can be taken as mine, no sense of self, in that long, infinitely everlasting thing, can ever be maintained anymore.

74
00:07:02,757 --> 00:07:09,640
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're seeing, as in common to every experience on the more particular level, what you're seeing on the more general level, is the five aggregates, or one among them.

75
00:07:10,637 --> 00:07:12,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Just different aspects of your experience.

76
00:07:12,819 --> 00:07:15,601
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, as a structure, they always have to be there.

77
00:07:15,641 --> 00:07:22,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, as a structure, they will not be discerned, even as a structure, if those particular things are not arisen beforehand, not given.

78
00:07:25,369 --> 00:07:31,294
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it feeds itself, mutually determined, depending on where you look at.

79
00:07:32,835 --> 00:07:36,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And that that center, sense of self, that it's kind of in the center, it sort of feels like

80
00:07:38,048 --> 00:07:38,748
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always there.

81
00:07:39,128 --> 00:07:41,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always seeing these things, witnessing these things, or whatever else.

82
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:44,351
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to deny that.

83
00:07:44,391 --> 00:07:46,652
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to try to remove that, in a sense of this is wrong.

84
00:07:46,692 --> 00:07:47,812
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's there, as such.

85
00:07:49,033 --> 00:07:56,276
[SPEAKER_01]: All you have to do is see that, also, as something that has arisen on its own, in result to other things that have already arisen on its own.

86
00:07:56,776 --> 00:08:00,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, even that sense of self, you can't own.

87
00:08:01,558 --> 00:08:03,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly... Because if you would

88
00:08:05,284 --> 00:08:11,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, in theory, if you were to truly be the master of that sense of self, if you tell it to stop arising, it should stop arising.

89
00:08:13,032 --> 00:08:16,195
[SPEAKER_01]: If you tell it to rise like this, it should rise like that.

90
00:08:17,356 --> 00:08:23,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, impermanence is found right in, like, in the heart of non-mastery things.

91
00:08:24,583 --> 00:08:26,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're not in control.

92
00:08:27,072 --> 00:08:28,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You're arising, you're not controlling.

93
00:08:28,813 --> 00:08:35,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can be in control of, like, I think we spoke about a few days ago, to various different options that presents to you.

94
00:08:35,577 --> 00:08:37,258
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, when it comes down to choosing.

95
00:08:37,758 --> 00:08:39,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you can choose this or this or that or that.

96
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:48,344
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you cannot choose or not choose is the whole structure of choice that you are basically, well, imprisoned by.

97
00:08:48,984 --> 00:08:50,205
[SPEAKER_01]: You're within it, you have no choice.

98
00:08:50,385 --> 00:08:51,365
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, I'm not going to choose.

99
00:08:51,725 --> 00:08:52,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You just chose.

100
00:08:53,406 --> 00:08:54,787
[SPEAKER_01]: I refrain from any form of

101
00:08:55,295 --> 00:08:56,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice or action?

102
00:08:56,315 --> 00:08:57,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a form of choice.

103
00:08:58,276 --> 00:08:59,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will keep revolving around it.

104
00:08:59,997 --> 00:09:02,197
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of it through that choice.

105
00:09:03,198 --> 00:09:15,322
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can step outside, through that kind of fully enclosed picture, is if you start discerning it from inside as something that actually, well, cannot be yours.

106
00:09:15,642 --> 00:09:21,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, no matter what choices you make within that, can't pertain to you either.

107
00:09:22,473 --> 00:09:26,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor the result of those can eventually pertain to you either, because you abandon the whole thing.

108
00:09:26,314 --> 00:09:34,217
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is only possible once the moral behavior, the restraint and sīla have been developed and purified beforehand.

109
00:09:34,737 --> 00:09:39,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, if you just take it abstractly, you can say, okay, so it doesn't matter what I do doesn't apply to me.

110
00:09:39,779 --> 00:09:40,219
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it does.

111
00:09:40,939 --> 00:09:42,880
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you're bound by it, it applies to you.

112
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:45,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there is no the beginning.

113
00:09:45,681 --> 00:09:49,142
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no discernible beginning of when these things started applying to you.

114
00:09:50,688 --> 00:09:52,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it is gratuitous setup.

115
00:09:52,869 --> 00:09:56,352
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Abhijā is a gratuitous parasite, as Dīnabhira called it.

116
00:09:57,112 --> 00:10:00,854
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it shouldn't be there, but the fact that it's there, means it's there.

117
00:10:01,315 --> 00:10:06,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you undo it, the only reason you're able to undo it, is because it was never meant to be there.

118
00:10:06,558 --> 00:10:11,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that it was never meant to be there, you placed it there, and you maintain it there.

119
00:10:11,921 --> 00:10:14,823
[SPEAKER_01]: When you discern that actually it cannot be there, it's not there.

120
00:10:18,070 --> 00:10:32,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through purification of the behavior of the body, the speech and the mind, then, eventually, you recognize that no matter what options of your choice are given to you, the nature of the choice, it's not in your control.

121
00:10:34,198 --> 00:10:39,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can only choose is good, bad or neutral.

122
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:43,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you focus on choosing good.

123
00:10:45,097 --> 00:10:46,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Or wholesome, rather.

124
00:10:46,418 --> 00:10:50,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily, like, good, good, what's accepted, commonly cited as good.

125
00:10:50,241 --> 00:10:51,361
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is wholesome?

126
00:10:51,381 --> 00:10:53,823
[SPEAKER_01]: What is non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion?

127
00:10:53,923 --> 00:10:54,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you choose.

128
00:10:54,864 --> 00:10:56,925
[SPEAKER_00]: There are three choices.

129
00:10:57,125 --> 00:10:58,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Three choices for it.

130
00:10:58,346 --> 00:11:00,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

131
00:11:00,908 --> 00:11:05,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, yeah, there are only three directions.

132
00:11:05,431 --> 00:11:07,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, within those directions, there are more directions, and so on.

133
00:11:08,735 --> 00:11:11,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what confuses people in thinking they own things.

134
00:11:11,457 --> 00:11:18,882
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, that you were thrown within that center, within that inevitability of choice, means you don't really own that either.

135
00:11:19,722 --> 00:11:26,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what particular choice you make on the result of that inevitability of that choice, it doesn't really matter.

136
00:11:27,408 --> 00:11:28,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, fundamentally.

137
00:11:28,588 --> 00:11:31,010
[SPEAKER_01]: It matters practically.

138
00:11:31,450 --> 00:11:38,455
[SPEAKER_01]: It matters on a particular level, whether it's wholesome or unwholesome, whether it's going to contribute to the maintenance of that, like, delusional view.

139
00:11:39,275 --> 00:11:41,537
[SPEAKER_01]: or undermining of the delusional view.

140
00:11:42,057 --> 00:11:43,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it matters in that sense.

141
00:11:43,178 --> 00:11:48,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But fundamentally, from the point of view of the five aggregates, it doesn't really matter, because the nature of the choice is there.

142
00:11:49,083 --> 00:11:55,148
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why somebody who does understand that nature of choice and abandonment, he goes beyond good and bad, beyond wholesome and unwholesome.

143
00:12:07,577 --> 00:12:08,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't have to go, like,

144
00:12:09,608 --> 00:12:10,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not hard to look for it.

145
00:12:10,989 --> 00:12:12,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Just take up any experience.

146
00:12:12,109 --> 00:12:13,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Take the experience right here, right now.

147
00:12:14,170 --> 00:12:16,351
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sense of... call it self.

148
00:12:16,471 --> 00:12:17,372
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sense of self.

149
00:12:17,412 --> 00:12:25,956
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sense of the... that which is in common... that... that which is in common to all the different experiences you can get through... through... through different senses.

150
00:12:26,476 --> 00:12:30,118
[SPEAKER_01]: The... the center of the experience, the... the... the knee or the point of view,

151
00:12:31,058 --> 00:12:37,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you're more spiritually inclined, you can call it the observer, as I said before, the pure awareness, the knower, whatever else.

152
00:12:38,263 --> 00:12:39,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Call it whatever you like.

153
00:12:41,205 --> 00:12:43,266
[SPEAKER_01]: If you take it to be yours, that's a problem.

154
00:12:44,507 --> 00:12:47,288
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't take it to be yours, that's a solution.

155
00:12:48,609 --> 00:12:50,270
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that thing can still remain.

156
00:12:51,311 --> 00:12:52,772
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... because... because it's inevitable.

157
00:12:52,792 --> 00:12:58,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when there are things, the five aggregates, and when there is a point of view, they will have... it has to be centered somewhere.

158
00:12:59,612 --> 00:13:03,754
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you end up assuming the nature of that center as permanent.

159
00:13:04,034 --> 00:13:05,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you take it out to be yours.

160
00:13:06,536 --> 00:13:09,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no center if there are no other things defining the center.

161
00:13:10,778 --> 00:13:13,740
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive, oh, this is in the center of what?

162
00:13:14,820 --> 00:13:18,442
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is nothing defining it, center will not be discerned at all.

163
00:13:19,002 --> 00:13:20,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It just will not emerge.

164
00:13:21,428 --> 00:13:24,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how... and that center can always look the same, sure.

165
00:13:25,210 --> 00:13:27,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the center of different things.

166
00:13:27,732 --> 00:13:31,414
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the fact that there are different things, that's what defines the center.

167
00:13:32,935 --> 00:13:36,497
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even your sense of self, then, is defined by all the other particular things.

168
00:13:37,178 --> 00:13:48,605
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, if that sense of self is taken to be mine, to be my own self, that's defined by all other particular things that you own, that you took to be mine.

169
00:13:49,878 --> 00:14:00,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Implicitly, a little bit, a little bit here, a little bit there, they all contribute to them, this center of self, that's in common to all of them, feels as like really mine, really personal, really dear to me.

170
00:14:02,681 --> 00:14:10,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what needs to be removed out of it, not the actual sense of the center of the experience, the ownership of it.

171
00:14:12,244 --> 00:14:13,184
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem there.

172
00:14:13,564 --> 00:14:18,025
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove that ownership and then it's what you have left, it's what you would have called individuality.

173
00:14:18,627 --> 00:14:19,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the center of experience.

174
00:14:20,047 --> 00:14:20,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

175
00:14:21,408 --> 00:14:30,931
[SPEAKER_01]: The center of that, those five aggregates, the most common center, the most the most prevailing characteristic of a center of those five aggregates.

176
00:14:31,711 --> 00:14:33,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's different to different people.

177
00:14:35,232 --> 00:14:36,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, different individuals.

178
00:14:37,353 --> 00:14:42,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's the same, let's say, if it's two individuals the same, the fact is, there are two, thus they're different.

179
00:14:42,875 --> 00:14:43,935
[SPEAKER_01]: There's one, there's two.

180
00:14:44,850 --> 00:14:47,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they're completely mirrored, it's still two.

181
00:14:47,572 --> 00:14:50,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's two different things, two different individuals.

182
00:14:51,834 --> 00:14:59,738
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, you can't have, like, two five aggregates to be identical, because if they would be identical, they would be indistinguishable.

183
00:15:01,499 --> 00:15:01,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

184
00:15:12,735 --> 00:15:14,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, a place whereby things are experienced.

185
00:15:15,957 --> 00:15:16,338
[SPEAKER_00]: A place.

186
00:15:17,619 --> 00:15:19,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, no, not quite.

187
00:15:20,521 --> 00:15:21,681
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not experiencing it.

188
00:15:24,003 --> 00:15:27,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But this experience of things generates the sense of the center.

189
00:15:32,950 --> 00:15:33,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

190
00:15:35,752 --> 00:15:36,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

191
00:15:37,042 --> 00:15:38,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it depends how you define here.

192
00:15:38,683 --> 00:15:42,784
[SPEAKER_01]: If you define here as opposite of yonder, then it's not here, because you won't find it here.

193
00:15:43,404 --> 00:15:50,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It's including... it has to include both, so it would be there.

194
00:15:54,949 --> 00:15:58,950
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that center, it's in regard to particular experiences.

195
00:16:01,211 --> 00:16:03,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're not in the center, nor is the center in the

196
00:16:06,702 --> 00:16:17,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just when there are senses, there is a center, there's a sense of a center in regards to senses, which is exactly how the mind and the senses are kind of arranged, so to speak.

197
00:16:18,169 --> 00:16:19,130
[SPEAKER_01]: They meet in the mind.

198
00:16:19,670 --> 00:16:20,550
[SPEAKER_01]: They all center in it.

199
00:16:20,671 --> 00:16:21,551
[SPEAKER_01]: They all gather there.

200
00:16:22,832 --> 00:16:27,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see that even the arrangement of the six senses, the five senses and the mind is the sixth,

201
00:16:28,486 --> 00:16:38,531
[SPEAKER_01]: All it implies, already, already reflects that structure of, like, these particular things generating meeting up in this center.

202
00:16:39,071 --> 00:16:42,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the mind will simply not be discerned if there are no five senses.

203
00:16:43,813 --> 00:16:45,314
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be nothing defining it.

204
00:16:46,935 --> 00:16:52,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there is, if there is no... Well, it's not, it's not a perfect analogy, but it can give you the idea.

205
00:16:52,757 --> 00:16:54,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If there are no bricks,

206
00:16:55,200 --> 00:16:58,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Consisting, making this little house, you will have a house.

207
00:17:00,003 --> 00:17:02,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly will not have a center in that house.

208
00:17:02,464 --> 00:17:03,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the center of the house.

209
00:17:04,525 --> 00:17:06,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you remove all the definition of it.

210
00:17:08,828 --> 00:17:11,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, the mind is defined through these five senses.

211
00:17:11,649 --> 00:17:21,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, at the same time, these five senses, well, they will simply not be operable in any way, if there is no mind, if there is no... their recognition in the mind.

212
00:17:25,042 --> 00:17:32,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even that structure already kind of implies the idea of ownership.

213
00:17:32,389 --> 00:17:39,735
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see how it's not hard to end up assuming the sense of self, the owner, the soul, the me, I am.

214
00:17:41,217 --> 00:17:48,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Because things are arranged in that way and then you just have to kind of put that last little touch to it and make it mine.

215
00:17:49,305 --> 00:17:56,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to go against that grain, against that seeming kind of arrangement of the self in the center, which is basically a deception.

216
00:18:07,568 --> 00:18:10,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And you go against it, not by denying this center of experience.

217
00:18:10,789 --> 00:18:10,989
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

218
00:18:11,189 --> 00:18:14,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can take I am...

219
00:18:14,429 --> 00:18:14,849
[SPEAKER_01]: I am me.

220
00:18:15,170 --> 00:18:15,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I am myself.

221
00:18:16,130 --> 00:18:17,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You can take that as a valid phenomenon.

222
00:18:19,227 --> 00:18:19,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You are you.

223
00:18:19,787 --> 00:18:20,568
[SPEAKER_01]: You are yourself.

224
00:18:21,228 --> 00:18:23,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, don't take that to be yours.

225
00:18:23,909 --> 00:18:27,991
[SPEAKER_01]: That whole experience of me, I am, you disown that.

226
00:18:29,111 --> 00:18:39,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you disown that personal center, the personality aspects, because there's no ownership in regard to it, and you just have the individual left, left behind.

227
00:18:39,655 --> 00:18:39,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Experience.

228
00:18:40,335 --> 00:18:40,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience.

229
00:18:41,496 --> 00:18:43,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience of these very five aggregates.

230
00:18:43,677 --> 00:18:44,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience of this point of view.

231
00:18:45,037 --> 00:18:46,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience of these preferences.

232
00:18:47,642 --> 00:18:49,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why ārahants are indistinguishable.

233
00:18:49,903 --> 00:18:52,023
[SPEAKER_01]: External practitioners can't tell them apart.

234
00:18:52,223 --> 00:18:52,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Why wouldn't they?

235
00:18:53,804 --> 00:18:56,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Individual... You can be individual without being a person.

236
00:18:56,444 --> 00:18:59,765
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't be a person without already including the individuality in it.

237
00:19:01,246 --> 00:19:02,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm doing the personal.

238
00:19:03,246 --> 00:19:06,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, if you appropriate your individuality, you become a person.

239
00:19:07,567 --> 00:19:11,989
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that you are already a person means that appropriation is already there.

240
00:19:12,869 --> 00:19:13,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Beginningless.

241
00:19:15,330 --> 00:19:16,990
[SPEAKER_01]: But as you can see, it is gratuitous.

242
00:19:21,432 --> 00:19:24,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, remove the ownership of your person, of your sense of personality.

243
00:19:25,673 --> 00:19:28,714
[SPEAKER_01]: However vague or general it might be, you remove the ownership of it.

244
00:19:28,954 --> 00:19:30,395
[SPEAKER_01]: What you have left is the individual.

245
00:19:31,275 --> 00:19:34,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And you remove the ownership of it by seeing that you can't really control it.

246
00:19:34,116 --> 00:19:34,996
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't belong to you.

247
00:19:35,476 --> 00:19:37,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That it could cease at any given moment.

248
00:19:38,797 --> 00:19:42,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Enough to make you let go of it.

249
00:19:42,938 --> 00:19:49,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Not only do it and deny its existence, but certainly stop holding it as yours, as something you are in charge of.

250
00:19:54,463 --> 00:20:08,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, impermanence is... if you want to translate it as impermanence, it's basically more closely related to the things might cease, than actually seeing them cease.

251
00:20:10,266 --> 00:20:19,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that you, your own sense of self, might cease, that's enough to discern the nature of impermanence pertaining to it.

252
00:20:22,088 --> 00:20:23,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can't see that might.

253
00:20:25,128 --> 00:20:30,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, you need thing to be present in order to see that it's possible for it to come to an end.

254
00:20:31,431 --> 00:20:34,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you recognize the possibility, that's it.

255
00:20:34,152 --> 00:20:36,373
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the nature of impermanence.

256
00:20:37,794 --> 00:20:42,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it was permanent, you can't imagine how that is.

257
00:20:42,696 --> 00:20:48,118
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't imagine a thing with... to be... to exist without a possibility to not be.

258
00:20:48,338 --> 00:20:48,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

259
00:20:48,698 --> 00:20:49,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is.

260
00:20:50,913 --> 00:20:55,735
[SPEAKER_01]: See, basically, being becomes the reason for its impermanence.

261
00:20:56,195 --> 00:20:56,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

262
00:20:56,635 --> 00:20:57,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising.

263
00:20:57,175 --> 00:21:03,897
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it has a reason, that's why the possibility of its cessation is inherently there, in that nature of that arising.

264
00:21:07,759 --> 00:21:09,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to see them cease at all.

265
00:21:10,139 --> 00:21:12,380
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to recognize them arising.

266
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:18,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you discern that nature, you will see that there's the inherent, implicit,

267
00:21:19,101 --> 00:21:23,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibility of them to not be, simply because they are right now.

268
00:21:24,404 --> 00:21:26,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And that possibility, that's enough.

269
00:21:26,625 --> 00:21:33,068
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense of possibility to suffer in the future, it's suffering in itself right now, that possibility.

270
00:21:34,229 --> 00:21:36,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you might feel great today, tomorrow, whatever.

271
00:21:36,850 --> 00:21:37,911
[SPEAKER_01]: You might feel great right now.

272
00:21:38,171 --> 00:21:43,174
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think that next week there might be something that will cause you to suffer some, that's already a form of worry.

273
00:21:43,694 --> 00:21:45,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And worry is unpleasant right here, right now.

274
00:21:48,913 --> 00:21:50,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it pertains to the same thing.

275
00:21:50,374 --> 00:21:51,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing that...

276
00:21:51,774 --> 00:21:52,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I am here.

277
00:21:52,814 --> 00:21:54,295
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm alive, right here, right now.

278
00:21:55,695 --> 00:21:57,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the next week, I might not be.

279
00:21:58,196 --> 00:21:58,536
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

280
00:21:58,876 --> 00:21:59,657
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes.

281
00:22:01,337 --> 00:22:05,278
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a whole... Where... How long has this been going on for?

282
00:22:06,039 --> 00:22:07,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, how long will it go on for?

283
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:09,940
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly... Yeah, that doubt.

284
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:10,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

285
00:22:10,240 --> 00:22:11,020
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that little doubt.

286
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:12,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that little doubt.

287
00:22:12,081 --> 00:22:13,241
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you look at it...

288
00:22:13,261 --> 00:22:13,621
[SPEAKER_01]: It should show.

289
00:22:14,101 --> 00:22:16,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, if you look at it even closer, you can see, oh, how long has this been...

290
00:22:16,682 --> 00:22:17,342
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

291
00:22:17,483 --> 00:22:17,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

292
00:22:19,943 --> 00:22:24,426
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as it has been going on for, there was always possibility for it to not be.

293
00:22:25,467 --> 00:22:29,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that reason, for as long as it was going on for, it was impermanent.

294
00:22:42,938 --> 00:22:47,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it was impermanent in that sense, you have no control over it.

295
00:22:47,501 --> 00:22:48,762
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was suffering.

296
00:22:53,018 --> 00:22:56,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, as if you have no control over possibilities.

297
00:22:56,980 --> 00:23:03,725
[SPEAKER_01]: I can control this or that, but I can't control all possible possibilities that might befall this experience.

298
00:23:03,765 --> 00:23:07,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just inconceivable, because control is secondary to those possibilities.

299
00:23:07,827 --> 00:23:09,668
[SPEAKER_01]: It's defined by the possibilities.

300
00:23:09,768 --> 00:23:14,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Your choice, which is a synonym for people, for putrugenas, a synonym with control.

301
00:23:15,252 --> 00:23:18,114
[SPEAKER_01]: It's defined by things that are possible or not possible.

302
00:23:18,474 --> 00:23:19,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't choose that which is not possible.

303
00:23:20,888 --> 00:23:23,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, those possibilities are very uncertain.

304
00:23:24,490 --> 00:23:29,972
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they undermine not just your present experience, but even your possible choice in regard to those possibilities.

305
00:23:30,412 --> 00:23:31,893
[SPEAKER_01]: It's determined by the possibilities.

306
00:23:32,794 --> 00:23:37,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, usually, people could discern possibilities based on the present experience.

307
00:23:37,776 --> 00:23:39,016
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the way it has to be.

308
00:23:39,997 --> 00:23:46,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you discern, it becomes apparent that, structurally, possibilities precede the experience as it has arisen.

309
00:23:48,064 --> 00:23:49,025
[SPEAKER_01]: They're more primordial.

310
00:23:49,045 --> 00:23:50,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they're simultaneously present.

311
00:23:50,706 --> 00:23:53,767
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean the possibilities come first and then these things follow later.

312
00:23:54,568 --> 00:24:03,673
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they simultaneously arise, but possibilities are more general in their nature, which means they're, well, deeper, so to speak, in terms of the structure.

313
00:24:04,134 --> 00:24:09,957
[SPEAKER_01]: They're more fundamental to the nature of a possible.

314
00:24:13,399 --> 00:24:14,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what impermanence is.

315
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:16,041
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what nature of suffering is.

316
00:24:18,415 --> 00:24:26,740
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's seen as such, means right here, right now, this center, this me, this self, will not be mine, will not feel mine.

317
00:25:04,046 --> 00:25:12,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it possible for you, sitting right here, right now, is it possible for you to die in the next two minutes?

318
00:25:13,153 --> 00:25:14,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in the next in-breath?

319
00:25:15,495 --> 00:25:16,216
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

320
00:25:16,276 --> 00:25:16,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it possible?

321
00:25:16,896 --> 00:25:17,577
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

322
00:25:19,007 --> 00:25:21,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, you do know if it's possible or not.

323
00:25:21,229 --> 00:25:22,950
[SPEAKER_01]: What you don't know... That's what I'm saying.

324
00:25:23,270 --> 00:25:24,291
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no doubt.

325
00:25:24,311 --> 00:25:25,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there's not even a doubt out.

326
00:25:25,712 --> 00:25:28,494
[SPEAKER_01]: There's just being being kind of obstinately blind.

327
00:25:29,935 --> 00:25:32,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you look at it closely, you don't know that you might die.

328
00:25:33,137 --> 00:25:36,539
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, the fact is that you don't know that you might die means you might die.

329
00:25:36,579 --> 00:25:37,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's good.

330
00:25:38,721 --> 00:25:40,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that you might die...

331
00:25:41,502 --> 00:25:46,545
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it is 50-50, say, or the fact that it's like one percentage you might die.

332
00:25:46,605 --> 00:25:49,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, there is one chance in a hundred that you might be dead.

333
00:25:50,407 --> 00:25:57,731
[SPEAKER_01]: That's enough to see this very center of yourself right here, right now, as you speak, as you doubt, as you think about death, as not being yours.

334
00:25:59,673 --> 00:26:01,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being able to be yours.

335
00:26:02,234 --> 00:26:07,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were truly yours, there would be simply no possibility of death pertaining to it.

336
00:26:12,773 --> 00:26:13,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can doubt.

337
00:26:14,934 --> 00:26:24,676
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to doubt whether you can die next moment or not, it's better to turn around, turn... sorry, turn it, turn it differently, and then doubt whether you're going to be alive the next moment or not.

338
00:26:24,876 --> 00:26:25,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the same thing.

339
00:26:26,676 --> 00:26:32,858
[SPEAKER_01]: It is obviously, you can see, by doubting whether I'm mortal or not, you give priority to a sense of self.

340
00:26:34,098 --> 00:26:38,319
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you doubt whether I'm mortal or not, you give priority to death.

341
00:26:39,299 --> 00:26:40,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You give priority to a non-sense of self.

342
00:26:41,720 --> 00:26:42,540
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing.

343
00:26:44,164 --> 00:26:46,086
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said it in the same way for that reason.

344
00:26:46,206 --> 00:26:56,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, you can doubt whether you're going to die or you can doubt if you're going to be alive.

345
00:26:58,876 --> 00:27:00,998
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it possible for you to be alive next moment?

346
00:27:02,279 --> 00:27:02,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe not, yeah.

347
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:03,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

348
00:27:08,804 --> 00:27:09,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maybe not.

349
00:27:10,285 --> 00:27:10,686
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

350
00:27:10,766 --> 00:27:11,947
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what impermanence is.

351
00:27:12,474 --> 00:27:16,197
[SPEAKER_00]: But I might not be alive, rather than I might die.

352
00:27:16,537 --> 00:27:21,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you have knowledge to it properly, then you can ask either way.

353
00:27:21,502 --> 00:27:26,406
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason why I ask it differently is because, yeah, you said like many people would say, well, I don't know.

354
00:27:27,667 --> 00:27:28,327
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm here now.

355
00:27:28,548 --> 00:27:31,050
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, maybe that experience of death, I'll still be here.

356
00:27:31,630 --> 00:27:32,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe that's it.

357
00:27:33,232 --> 00:27:36,755
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it's maybe for you right here, right now, means maybe not.

358
00:27:38,196 --> 00:27:40,698
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it means maybe not, it means you're not in control of it.

359
00:27:41,367 --> 00:27:47,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're not in control of it, it can't be yours in the future, as a possibility, and certainly not yours right here, right now.

360
00:27:47,655 --> 00:27:51,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can look, well, it was certainly not mine even in the past.

361
00:27:51,359 --> 00:27:53,382
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Arhat's knowledge attends to.

362
00:27:54,797 --> 00:27:58,920
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the present knowledge pertains and pervades his past and the future.

363
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:05,184
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't even think, even before he was an arahant, when he remembers his patujana time, he doesn't think, I was back then either.

364
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:11,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that sense of abandoning ownership of your sense of self, it just pervades the whole structure.

365
00:28:11,568 --> 00:28:15,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Present, past is present, and future is present, and present is present.

366
00:28:20,683 --> 00:28:30,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say this center-self, this center of my experience, just feels so real, so permanent, it's just eternal, been around all the time.

367
00:28:34,234 --> 00:28:36,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And that all might be valid, in terms of how it feels.

368
00:28:38,078 --> 00:28:39,038
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up for debate.

369
00:28:39,799 --> 00:28:44,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But what it is, what is up for debate, is if it's going to stay like that or not.

370
00:28:45,624 --> 00:28:46,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you assume,

371
00:28:47,402 --> 00:28:51,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Since all I can remember, it was this sense of self, thus this sense of self will continue.

372
00:28:51,963 --> 00:28:53,683
[SPEAKER_01]: That will be an assumption, always.

373
00:28:55,484 --> 00:28:57,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, well, maybe it won't.

374
00:28:57,824 --> 00:29:03,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And that maybe is enough for you to discern, if you look at it closely enough, the impermanence of it.

375
00:29:04,165 --> 00:29:08,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because there is that the tiny little thread of maybe in it.

376
00:29:09,366 --> 00:29:16,428
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start looking at it closer, well, longer and longer, it... it... well, it would be less and less of a thread.

377
00:29:17,413 --> 00:29:24,219
[SPEAKER_01]: It will become more and more like a proper foundation of the experience, the full-on structure of that maybe.

378
00:29:26,881 --> 00:29:30,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And if everything sits upon maybe, I can't own it.

379
00:29:31,224 --> 00:29:34,367
[SPEAKER_00]: I can own only that which was given beforehand.

380
00:29:35,748 --> 00:29:40,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, I can entertain the idea of ownership in regard to that which was given beforehand.

381
00:29:41,045 --> 00:29:49,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means even that entertained idea of ownership is the result of that which was given beforehand, which was already affected by that maybe.

382
00:29:49,930 --> 00:29:53,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, my whole ownership is not really mine, because it could have been otherwise.

383
00:29:55,293 --> 00:30:10,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the, the,

384
00:30:12,425 --> 00:30:15,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not mine right here, right now, because it was affected by that maybe.

385
00:30:16,308 --> 00:30:19,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever it's in the future, it will certainly not be mine.

386
00:30:20,731 --> 00:30:22,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's mine, so to speak.

387
00:30:22,272 --> 00:30:27,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it has a reason for me, given... and I put everything as my choice, it will not be for me.

388
00:30:30,058 --> 00:30:35,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was affected by that inherent possibility of maybe not.

389
00:30:37,503 --> 00:30:40,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything that arises is maybe.

390
00:30:41,761 --> 00:30:43,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe for you, maybe not.

391
00:30:43,422 --> 00:30:49,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that which has arisen as for you, it's not for you either, because it could have been not.

392
00:30:51,229 --> 00:30:57,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's a similar one when the Buddha asked the man, are there more things that you haven't seen or the things that you've seen?

393
00:30:58,455 --> 00:31:01,058
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, of course, well, there are more things that I haven't seen.

394
00:31:01,178 --> 00:31:03,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there more things that you haven't heard than ones that you heard?

395
00:31:03,840 --> 00:31:06,282
[SPEAKER_01]: More things that you haven't tasted than ones that you've tasted?

396
00:31:06,342 --> 00:31:09,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, there are more things that haven't arisen

397
00:31:10,777 --> 00:31:17,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for that very reason, you abandon everything that has a reason.

398
00:31:17,938 --> 00:31:22,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that, like, has a reason in the past, everything that arises now, and everything that will arise in the future.

399
00:31:22,759 --> 00:31:22,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

400
00:31:23,039 --> 00:31:26,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those things that haven't a reason will always remain more numerous.

401
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:38,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you go back to what I spoke before, what I just said about this possibility of maybe not, maybe yes, maybe it will persist, maybe it will arise in the same way, maybe it won't.

402
00:31:39,116 --> 00:31:43,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, whatever does arise, there will always be a possibility of it not being.

403
00:31:43,498 --> 00:31:45,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Those possibilities will be always more numerous.

404
00:31:46,299 --> 00:31:51,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the possibility of maybe not is actually much vaster than everything that has arisen.

405
00:31:51,241 --> 00:31:53,142
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start looking at your whole experience.

406
00:31:54,783 --> 00:32:01,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say, you... not just this life, but you can... all of the lives you've been around, you can... you can... you can look at the whole thing as completely circumstantial.

407
00:32:02,826 --> 00:32:05,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Arisen, but it could have not.

408
00:32:05,788 --> 00:32:08,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And that could have not was much vast, much greater.

409
00:32:09,683 --> 00:32:13,964
[SPEAKER_01]: than that which did manifest at some point in time.

410
00:32:15,964 --> 00:32:17,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can think about the future as well.

411
00:32:18,505 --> 00:32:29,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever does arise, if you do continue living for a while, whatever does arise, it will arise on the ground of that much vaster non-arising.

412
00:32:30,608 --> 00:32:35,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, whatever is given, no matter how great, how how lonely, or how painful, how frightening,

413
00:32:37,115 --> 00:32:41,697
[SPEAKER_01]: It could have not been, and that could have not been, and it wouldn't have been for me.

414
00:32:41,877 --> 00:32:42,677
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much greater.

415
00:32:44,078 --> 00:32:55,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it could have not been, and that which did come to be, it's certainly not for me, because it came on the basis of all the things that haven't even manifested.

416
00:32:56,303 --> 00:33:01,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, that which manifests, it's completely circumstantial, determined by all of it that didn't manifest.

417
00:33:02,215 --> 00:33:06,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it certainly cannot be mine, or for me, or special, in that sense.

418
00:33:06,676 --> 00:33:08,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Because every sense of ownership is special.

419
00:33:08,477 --> 00:33:08,917
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mine.

420
00:33:09,937 --> 00:33:10,037
[SPEAKER_01]: I.

421
00:33:10,378 --> 00:33:17,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even if you take a sense of self, it means, yeah, that's why the conceit and ego revolve around that appropriation of your own sense of self.

422
00:33:17,280 --> 00:33:18,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel special.

423
00:33:19,140 --> 00:33:22,981
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at the whole thing, including your sense of self, completely circumstantial, random.

424
00:33:23,682 --> 00:33:24,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainism.

425
00:33:24,922 --> 00:33:25,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertain.

426
00:33:25,542 --> 00:33:26,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty.

427
00:33:26,282 --> 00:33:27,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty.

428
00:33:27,603 --> 00:33:28,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Unsure, maybe.

429
00:33:28,503 --> 00:33:28,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

430
00:33:30,204 --> 00:33:31,744
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, like,

431
00:33:32,266 --> 00:33:49,179
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure Ajahn Chah... well, I'm not sure, because I've never met him, but based on other things that I did read of his transcribed talks, I'm pretty positive that he meant it in the right way, when he said everything unsure.

432
00:33:49,659 --> 00:33:59,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, people now just say that as a sort of mantra, but as you can see, you need to do a bit of drilling and digging in order for this maybe or unsure

433
00:34:00,433 --> 00:34:02,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Or uncertain to start to apply correctly.

434
00:34:02,634 --> 00:34:04,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you'd just be denying everything.

435
00:34:04,375 --> 00:34:04,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, whatever.

436
00:34:04,736 --> 00:34:05,536
[SPEAKER_00]: What time are you coming?

437
00:34:05,556 --> 00:34:07,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm sure, I'm sure.

438
00:34:07,958 --> 00:34:09,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

439
00:34:09,158 --> 00:34:12,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you like something to eat today?

440
00:34:12,400 --> 00:34:12,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

441
00:34:12,761 --> 00:34:13,621
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

442
00:34:13,661 --> 00:34:14,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncertain about it.

443
00:34:14,642 --> 00:34:15,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm uncertain about it.

444
00:34:15,922 --> 00:34:18,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, are you uncertain about your uncertainty?

445
00:34:18,144 --> 00:34:23,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Because obviously... Yeah, because you can be taking your sense of uncertainty as me, as mine.

446
00:34:23,987 --> 00:34:24,187
[SPEAKER_01]: See?

447
00:34:24,688 --> 00:34:27,890
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not attached to anything and you're attached to the view if I'm not attached to anything.

448
00:34:28,150 --> 00:34:28,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

449
00:34:28,922 --> 00:34:32,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it takes a bit more than that, but yeah, yeah, it comes down to that.

450
00:34:32,664 --> 00:34:39,847
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it's... it was always affected by maybe means whatever has arisen, it was more likely to not be.

451
00:34:43,968 --> 00:34:49,691
[SPEAKER_01]: And because there's always a greater likelihood of things not being, you can... you can... you can then realize, well, there's nothing I can own.

452
00:34:50,271 --> 00:34:54,493
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing that will entertain, nothing that will escape this uncertainty.

453
00:34:55,918 --> 00:35:07,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's with me for hundreds of years, well, like devas, like they're asked for eons and eons, and yeah, sure, they forget that they were born, because they've been around for so long, their memory lapses, and they do think they're eternal for that reason.

454
00:35:08,507 --> 00:35:15,691
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not accidental that people then entertain the kind of notions of eternity when it comes to the sense of self and the soul.

455
00:35:16,452 --> 00:35:23,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, it always seems today, it's always like all these particular things might be arising or whatever, and I can see them being destroyed, but this sense of self remains there.

456
00:35:26,638 --> 00:35:30,361
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's always possibility for you to not remain there, and you don't look at that possibility.

457
00:35:30,741 --> 00:35:34,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if people start looking at possibility they might die, they start freaking out.

458
00:35:35,585 --> 00:35:41,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, going back to what I was saying, the possibility that I might die or death might happen to me.

459
00:35:43,584 --> 00:35:43,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

460
00:35:43,924 --> 00:35:48,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Then again, it might be it might be best to ask, am I still going to be alive?

461
00:35:48,526 --> 00:35:48,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

462
00:35:49,146 --> 00:35:50,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I say death, what happens?

463
00:35:50,086 --> 00:35:50,767
[SPEAKER_01]: It moves the center.

464
00:35:50,787 --> 00:35:52,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, shifts the center of the question.

465
00:35:52,647 --> 00:35:53,788
[SPEAKER_01]: That will happen to me.

466
00:35:53,808 --> 00:35:57,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it becomes... death becomes another external experience that you witness.

467
00:35:57,389 --> 00:35:58,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you remain permanent.

468
00:35:58,650 --> 00:36:00,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you remain unaffected by that death.

469
00:36:01,331 --> 00:36:02,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is exactly what people think.

470
00:36:02,831 --> 00:36:03,912
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, everybody dies.

471
00:36:04,052 --> 00:36:04,552
[SPEAKER_01]: I will die.

472
00:36:04,792 --> 00:36:07,393
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I'll experience something, and then it's kind of weird.

473
00:36:07,413 --> 00:36:13,035
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even death will be an experience of me with... experience that I'm witnessing, that my sense of self is witnessing.

474
00:36:13,055 --> 00:36:14,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, my sense of self will remain eternal.

475
00:36:15,095 --> 00:36:15,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

476
00:36:16,336 --> 00:36:19,416
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at, well, will my sense of self remain eternal?

477
00:36:20,937 --> 00:36:22,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, maybe.

478
00:36:23,518 --> 00:36:27,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that it's maybe, the fact that you can't give a hundred percent certain answer

479
00:36:28,846 --> 00:36:29,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Means that's a problem.

480
00:36:30,087 --> 00:36:32,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will never be able to give a hundred percent certain answer.

481
00:36:32,989 --> 00:36:33,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

482
00:36:33,209 --> 00:36:35,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can doubt it to begin with.

483
00:36:35,731 --> 00:36:39,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You can place it in a question by asking, am I?

484
00:36:40,534 --> 00:36:47,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are a hundred percent certain, if you were truly the owner, even the question of questioning that ownership would be inconceivable.

485
00:36:48,559 --> 00:36:52,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you would own the future in the same sense you're owning right here, right now, through the present.

486
00:36:54,793 --> 00:37:05,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you come to the answer, yes, I will live forever, the fact that you have asked that question beforehand, means that question of, or rather that possibility of maybe, precedes it.

487
00:37:05,419 --> 00:37:05,819
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there.

488
00:37:05,919 --> 00:37:10,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can... whatever you come up with as an answer, it's going to be an assumption.

489
00:37:11,162 --> 00:37:14,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... it was accessible to doubt.

490
00:37:15,985 --> 00:37:19,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doubting it, means you can't really own it.

491
00:37:19,227 --> 00:37:20,948
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no certainty in regard to it.

492
00:37:22,857 --> 00:37:29,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by doubting whether you're going to be alive, you recognize that, okay, I'm not in charge of that.

493
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:39,064
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you... if you convince yourself that you will always be... that your sense of self is eternal, you realize that can always be placed in question.

494
00:37:39,344 --> 00:37:49,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you refrain from doing it, the fact that there is a possibility of it being questioned, means it's subject to that maybe.

495
00:37:50,409 --> 00:37:52,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's subject to that maybe, then it's not certain.

496
00:37:54,209 --> 00:37:55,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what nitya is.

497
00:37:56,870 --> 00:38:14,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And any, anything, take any, any non-certainty, any uncertainty, sorry, take any uncertainty, take any uncertainty in regard to anything of that nature, it will always be suffering.

498
00:38:15,611 --> 00:38:23,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha, or rather, let's not call it suffering, because suffering would be an over-translation, in the same sense as impermanence would be an over-translation.

499
00:38:23,994 --> 00:38:26,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's call it unease.

500
00:38:27,435 --> 00:38:30,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Any uncertainty is also uneasy.

501
00:38:32,457 --> 00:38:40,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And if something is uncertain and uneasy, calling it like a form of concern, that cannot possibly be something you are in control of.

502
00:38:41,519 --> 00:38:45,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Because control is completely the opposite of... control is ease.

503
00:38:45,783 --> 00:38:46,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Control is ownership.

504
00:38:46,824 --> 00:38:47,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Control is mastery.

505
00:38:47,926 --> 00:38:48,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Control is control.

506
00:38:49,087 --> 00:38:49,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncertainty.

507
00:38:50,428 --> 00:38:51,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty, of course.

508
00:38:51,809 --> 00:38:52,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

509
00:38:52,090 --> 00:38:52,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Control.

510
00:38:52,630 --> 00:38:52,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

511
00:38:52,911 --> 00:38:55,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I'm in control, this is certain.

512
00:38:56,214 --> 00:39:04,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say you're driving a car, and it's like, well, it's uncertain that this car will always go where you sort of steer the wheel, means, oh, so I'm not really in control of it.

513
00:39:05,123 --> 00:39:05,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

514
00:39:07,190 --> 00:39:16,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if 99% of the time you are controlling the car, that 1% of it not listening to you means your control was undermined by that relativity.

515
00:39:17,072 --> 00:39:22,714
[SPEAKER_01]: All the time, at any given time of your control, it was always undermined by the possibility of not being your control.

516
00:39:23,314 --> 00:39:27,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it was never your control in that personal, eternal sense.

517
00:39:49,961 --> 00:39:51,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there it is, the formula.

518
00:39:52,282 --> 00:39:55,082
[SPEAKER_01]: It might not be, and it might not be for me.

519
00:39:55,602 --> 00:40:00,244
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not be, and it will not be for me.

520
00:40:00,264 --> 00:40:09,546
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, it might not be, and there will not be for me, means a thing is affected by that uncertainty, by that maybe.

521
00:40:11,246 --> 00:40:15,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever has arisen right here, right now, it could have not arisen.

522
00:40:16,824 --> 00:40:18,145
[SPEAKER_01]: means it was uncertain.

523
00:40:18,525 --> 00:40:20,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, it's manifested in a certain way, and that's certain.

524
00:40:22,588 --> 00:40:28,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But that whole certainty is undermined by this more general uncertainty that it could have... like, it could have easily not been.

525
00:40:29,852 --> 00:40:32,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can look at your experiences, like, whatever, your past.

526
00:40:32,734 --> 00:40:35,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever happened to you, it could have not happened.

527
00:40:36,256 --> 00:40:41,079
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, the bad things, everybody wishes that they wouldn't have not... they wouldn't have happened.

528
00:40:41,840 --> 00:40:43,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And the good things, obviously, you never question.

529
00:40:44,502 --> 00:40:45,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at the whole thing,

530
00:40:48,339 --> 00:40:57,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Impersonally, so to speak, you will recognize that, yeah, it, these things that have arisen, they could have equally not manifested itself.

531
00:40:59,567 --> 00:41:01,168
[SPEAKER_01]: So you had no say in their arising.

532
00:41:01,428 --> 00:41:07,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, so the formula that carries on, thus, it will not be and it will not be for me.

533
00:41:08,373 --> 00:41:14,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So you recognize that then in the future, whatever arises, it will be on a basis of it could have not arisen,

534
00:41:15,842 --> 00:41:16,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that...

535
00:41:17,322 --> 00:41:29,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it arises in the future, it might arise, sure, but one thing for sure, it will never arise for you, as yours, as mine, because it was always affected by that uncertainty.

536
00:41:30,705 --> 00:41:33,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things right here, right now, are affected by that uncertainty.

537
00:41:34,085 --> 00:41:42,507
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, things in the future that arise, if I live that long, let's say, they might arise, but one thing for sure is they will not arise as mine.

538
00:41:43,713 --> 00:41:47,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they were mine to begin with, they would never be uncertain.

539
00:41:47,617 --> 00:41:56,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you find uncertainty first, means whatever arises in the future, whatever experience you go through, it's circumstantial.

540
00:41:56,787 --> 00:41:58,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, it can never be yours.

541
00:41:58,850 --> 00:42:00,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can go through all sorts of experiences.

542
00:42:00,972 --> 00:42:01,753
[SPEAKER_01]: You can live for 200 years,

543
00:42:03,235 --> 00:42:08,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But one thing you will be certain of is that none of it will be arising for you, i.e.

544
00:42:09,118 --> 00:42:10,539
[SPEAKER_01]: for your sense of self, i.e.

545
00:42:10,619 --> 00:42:17,182
